            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. 



I recommend outfitting electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 



I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 



Other upgrades suggested in this report, such as insulating your crawl space, may also improve your air leakage. 



I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $400 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 11.1 GJ.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace walls are concrete with no insulation. The header space is also uninsulated, which is likely why the floor of your house is cold in the winter. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R20) rigid foam polyisocyanurate insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls. I recommend only insulating the walls that are exposed to the front, back, and garage of your house, as the side walls are common walls that are shared with your neighbours. 



As your crawlspace is heated, I have not modelled adding insulation to the floor above your crawlspace. However, I have modelled adding R20 to the header space between the floor joists.



This particular energy upgrade will result in energy savings, but will also make your home more comfortable to live in. 



Savings:

By adding 4 inches (R24) rigid foam polyisocyanurate insulation to the crawlspace walls, you could potentially save $530 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $280 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the crawl space wall area, including the header area, and increase its insulation value by a minimum of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane windows, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all your windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



While I have not modelled replacing your doors, if they need to be replaced look for ENERGY STAR?????? qualified doors rated for climate zone 3.



While you mentioned that you are not able to replace the doors and windows youself as the condo corporation takes care of those items, I have included this information so that you have it available to you. In the meantime, your windows are in good physical condition. 



Savings: 

By replacingall your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $180 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 5.0 GJ.   



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>



You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $1060 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 0.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 82.8% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE which also incorporates an electronically commutated motor (ECM) fan blower. If you choose a high-efficiency propane furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



If condo regulations require you to stay with oil, Arctic Energy Alliance recommends an oil furnace with 95% AFUE and an electronically commutated motor. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion propane-fired furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE which also incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $1,640 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 33.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

COMBUSTION AIR AND DEPRESSURIZATION

I did not see a combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the furnace. I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your furnace are being satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler is operating.



During my assessment of your home, the smoke detectors in your home appeared to be older models and I did not see a functioning carbon monoxide (CO) detector. Please ensure both types of detectors are installed and  operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ELECTRICITY USE - GENERAL

While I was on site, you mentioned that your electricity bills are very high. I did not see anything in particular that would cause this, but I recommend that you track your bills from month to month if you do not already. I also recommend that if you are plugging vehicles in during the winter that you use a timer on these plugins so that the vehicles are not drawing electricity all night. 



You can also borrow a Kill-a-Watt meter from either Arctic Energy Alliance or the Yellowknife Public Library to confirm what appliances in your home are using the most electricity. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Alexandra Giroux

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
